{"buzzwords":[{"author":"simon-jackson","date":"2025-09-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["bees","music","rhetoric","singing"],"id":"bees-monotone","content":"\nNot everyone in the early modern period seemed to have as much respect for the music of the bees as Butler. Roger Ascham uses what he perceives as the bees' monotone to criticise those who never learned to sing, and so struggle to achieve the full potential of rhetoric:\n\n> Where a matter is spoken, with an apte voyce, for euerye affection, the hearers for the moste parte, are moued as the speaker woulde. **But when a man is alwaye in one tune, lyke an Humble bee**....these shall neuer greatly mooue, as I haue known man wel learned, haue done, bicause theyr voyce was not stayed afore, with learnyng to synge.\n\nPoor unrhetorical bees!\n\n(Roger Ascham, Toxophilus, 1545, I, f.11v)"},{"author":"tiago","date":"2023-12-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["technology","connections","collaboration","methodology","buzzwords"],"id":"buzzword-infrastructure","content":"<script>\n    import { base } from \"$app/paths\";\n</script>\nThe biggest challenge in designing the **buzzword** system (in addition to the many technical challenges of creating filtering and searching options), was to create a system that would be able to balance the (mostly static, long-term and cost-efficient) technology with ease of use. It is crucial that everyone in the team is capable of adding **buzzwords** at any time, at will; but doing so in a user-friendly manner without using expensive content management systems is tricky. The solution we have now -- github based, markdown files -- is not perfect, but it will hopefully be easy enough for everyone. Only time will tell.\n![my view of the buzzword system]({base}/content/connections/buzzwords/img/buzzword_system.png)"},{"author":"olivia","date":"2025-04-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["honey","connections","science","flowers"],"id":"forensic-honey","content":"\nMolecular biologists have been studying whether honeybees could be useful in forensic science. Proteins in honey show which flowers bees have visited and the compounds in those flowers. Just as we can detect traces of plant pesticides in honey, so a team at George Mason University have been studying whether traces of human remains (volatile organic compounds) in honey could help with police investigations.   \n"},{"author":"olivia","date":"2024-01-29T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["text","connections","interdisciplinarity","butler","play","pleasure"],"title":"Play","id":"play","content":"\n>*Sometimes as well in Summer as Winter the Bees take pleasure to play abroad before the Hive, specially those that are in good plight, flying in and out, and about, so thicke, and so earnestly, as if they were swarming or fighting: when indeed it is onely to solace themselves:*\n\nAt the end of chapter one, Butler writes about the bees playing for pleasure and gives us the emotion-adjacent word 'solace.' The Oxford English Dictionary shows that the grammar of this word (which indicates comfort, consolation, entertainment, or recreation) can balance on the flow of energy between oneself and the world: you can give solace to others, give solace to yourself, or take solace. Here it means that the bees entertain themselves, though to what extent this is themselves individually or in a group dynamic is ambiguous.   \nThe punctuation mimics this play with its commas, which let us feel the pace of the bees' flight and give us a sense of their movement. In the 1609 edition the following paragraph, the last in the chapter, is typeset in a playful pyramid shape. \n"},{"author":"vivek","date":"2024-05-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["bees","differences","reading"],"id":"socialBees","content":"\nI've been reading about social learning in bees. They are more likley to approach flowers or locations occupied by other bees. Some of this is because they learn that bees indicate a reward, but even naive bees seem to be attracted to other bees. There's something special about others like them. I wonder how they realise a bee is a bee.\n"},{"author":"olivia","date":"2025-04-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["words","bee","etymology","social","connections"],"id":"spelling-bee","content":"\nApple bee, quilting bee, spelling bee. Why does American English use 'bee' to denote social events you can participate in which are not about bees at all? The *Oxford English Dictionary* says:\n\n> Origin uncertain.\n\n> Perhaps (i) a variant of English regional bean, been voluntary help from neighbours for the accomplishment of a particular task (perhaps itself a variant of boon n.1);\nor perhaps (ii) an extended use of bee n.1, with allusion to the social character of the insect.\n"},{"author":"olivia","date":"2025-04-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["wax","bees","company","charter","history"],"id":"wax-chandlers","content":"\nThe Worshipful Company of Wax Chandlers has a lot of interesting material on the history of wax laws, and they themselves have a very early modern history. [This page](https://www.waxchandlers.org.uk/about/wax-chandler-ordinances-of-1371/) explains all about the way early wax history interlinked with church history. The wax ordinance of 1371 guards against 'deceitful mixtures' and lists fines for the selling of adulterated wax.\n"}],"buzzwordTags":["bees","music","rhetoric","singing","technology","connections","collaboration","methodology","buzzwords","honey","science","flowers","text","interdisciplinarity","butler","play","pleasure","differences","reading","words","bee","etymology","social","wax","company","charter","history"],"buzzwordAuthors":["simon-jackson","tiago","olivia","vivek"]}